`printWindow.close()` can run **before the browser has finished opening or handling the print dialog**, closing the document too early. This is browser-dependent.

Close the window in the **`afterprint` event** instead:

```javascript
// Assume printWindow is the window you opened with window.open().
printWindow.document.open();
printWindow.document.write(`
    <!doctype html>
    <html>
        <head><title>Print</title></head>
        <body>toto</body>
    </html>
`);
printWindow.document.close();

// Register this BEFORE calling print().
printWindow.onafterprint = function () {
    printWindow.close();
};

printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();
// Do not call printWindow.close() here.
```

`afterprint` normally fires after printing has been initiated or print preview has been dismissed—including cancellation. It does **not** mean the physical printer has finished printing.

Also, use `document.open()` without `'text/plain'`; the content written into the window is HTML. If your actual document contains images or external stylesheets, wait for those to load before calling `print()`.

