Use **multiple `SecurityFilterChain` beans**, each with a `securityMatcher` that checks the `Referer` header. Spring Security uses the **first matching chain**, so order matters.

**Important:** `Referer` can be absent, shortened by browser policy, or forged by a client. Use it for optional behavior—not to decide whether a request is trusted or can bypass authentication.

### Example: Spring Boot 3 / Spring Security 6

This selects one filter for requests referred from `app-a.example.com`, another for `app-b.example.com`, and a default chain for everything else.

```java
import java.net.URI;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.core.annotation.Order;
import org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;
import org.springframework.security.web.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter;
import org.springframework.security.web.util.matcher.RequestMatcher;

@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    @Order(1)
    SecurityFilterChain appAChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(referredFrom("app-a.example.com"))
            .addFilterBefore(
                new AppAFilter(),
                UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class
            )
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    @Order(2)
    SecurityFilterChain appBChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .securityMatcher(referredFrom("app-b.example.com"))
            .addFilterBefore(
                new AppBFilter(),
                UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class
            )
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    @Order(3)
    SecurityFilterChain defaultChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }

    private static RequestMatcher referredFrom(String expectedHost) {
        return request -> {
            String referer = request.getHeader("Referer");

            if (referer == null) {
                return false;
            }

            try {
                URI uri = URI.create(referer);

                return "https".equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getScheme())
                    && expectedHost.equalsIgnoreCase(uri.getHost())
                    && (uri.getPort() == -1 || uri.getPort() == 443)
                    && uri.getRawUserInfo() == null;
            } catch (IllegalArgumentException ex) {
                return false;
            }
        };
    }
}
```

`AppAFilter` and `AppBFilter` are your custom filters, typically extending `OncePerRequestFilter`. Adjust their position depending on what they do; the example places them before the usual username/password authentication filter.

The matcher parses the URL and compares its host rather than using `contains()` or `startsWith()`, which could accidentally match something like `app-a.example.com.attacker.test`.

### Things to watch out for

- **Chains do not combine.** An App A request runs only the App A chain, not the default chain afterward. Put all required security configuration into each applicable chain.
- **Keep the fallback chain.** Otherwise, requests that match no chain are not protected by Spring Security.
- **Avoid double filter registration.** If your custom filters are Spring beans (`@Component` or `@Bean`), Spring Boot may also register them as servlet filters, causing them to run outside the selected security chain. Disable that registration with a `FilterRegistrationBean`, or instantiate them only within the security configuration as above.
- **If only the custom filter behavior differs**, keeping one chain and implementing `OncePerRequestFilter.shouldNotFilter()` in each filter is often simpler. Use separate chains when the broader security configurations differ too.

If this is intended to identify which frontend is making the request, use authenticated client identity or validated token claims instead of trusting `Referer`.

For a linked list, C++ smart pointers can automatically free nodes when they’re removed or when the list goes out of scope. This uses **RAII ownership**, rather than a tracing garbage collector.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

template <typename T>
class LinkedList {
    struct Node {
        T value;
        std::unique_ptr<Node> next;

        explicit Node(T value) : value(std::move(value)) {}
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Node> head_;

public:
    LinkedList() = default;

    // Each node has exactly one owner, so copying is disabled.
    LinkedList(const LinkedList&) = delete;
    LinkedList& operator=(const LinkedList&) = delete;

    ~LinkedList() {
        clear();
    }

    void push_front(T value) {
        auto node = std::make_unique<Node>(std::move(value));
        node->next = std::move(head_);
        head_ = std::move(node);
    }

    void pop_front() {
        if (!head_) {
            return;
        }

        auto oldHead = std::move(head_);
        head_ = std::move(oldHead->next);
        // oldHead is automatically deleted here.
    }

    void clear() {
        // Iterative cleanup avoids recursive destruction of a long list.
        while (head_) {
            pop_front();
        }
    }

    bool empty() const {
        return !head_;
    }

    void print() const {
        for (const Node* node = head_.get();
             node != nullptr;
             node = node->next.get()) {
            std::cout << node->value << " -> ";
        }
        std::cout << "null\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList<int> list;

    list.push_front(30);
    list.push_front(20);
    list.push_front(10);

    list.print();       // 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> null

    list.pop_front();   // Automatically frees the node containing 10.
    list.print();       // 20 -> 30 -> null
} // All remaining nodes are automatically freed.
```

Compile with C++14 or later:

```bash
g++ -std=c++14 main.cpp -o main
```

No manual `delete` is needed. For a doubly linked list, keep `next` owning and `prev` non-owning to avoid ownership cycles.

Most of the memory is used by this line in **every node**:

```cpp
node.resize(26, NULL);
```

On a typical 64-bit system, that allocates **208 bytes for pointers**, even if the node has only one child. There is also the `vector` object, allocation overhead, and `cnt`.

For better memory efficiency, store nodes in a single pool and represent children as a linked list of **integer indices**. This only stores children that actually exist.

### Memory-efficient implementation

```cpp
class Solution {
    struct Node {
        int firstChild = -1;
        int nextSibling = -1;
        int cnt = 0;
        char letter = '\0';
    };

    static int findChild(const std::vector<Node>& nodes,
                         int parent, char letter) {
        for (int child = nodes[parent].firstChild;
             child != -1;
             child = nodes[child].nextSibling) {
            if (nodes[child].letter == letter)
                return child;
        }
        return -1;
    }

public:
    std::vector<int> sumPrefixScores(std::vector<std::string>& words) {
        std::vector<Node> nodes(1); // Node 0 is the root.

        for (const std::string& word : words) {
            int current = 0;

            for (char c : word) {
                int child = findChild(nodes, current, c);

                if (child == -1) {
                    child = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());

                    Node newNode;
                    newNode.letter = c;
                    newNode.nextSibling = nodes[current].firstChild;

                    nodes.push_back(newNode);

                    // Use indices, not references: push_back can reallocate.
                    nodes[current].firstChild = child;
                }

                current = child;
                ++nodes[current].cnt;
            }
        }

        std::vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(words.size());

        for (const std::string& word : words) {
            int current = 0;
            int score = 0;

            for (char c : word) {
                // This child exists because every word was inserted above.
                current = findChild(nodes, current, c);
                score += nodes[current].cnt;
            }

            answer.push_back(score);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

### What improves

- **Much smaller nodes:** typically **16 bytes per node**, excluding unused vector capacity, instead of roughly **240 bytes plus allocation overhead** in your implementation.
- **No per-node heap allocation:** all nodes live in one vector.
- **No recursion:** avoids call-stack memory proportional to word length.
- **No string copies:** `const std::string&` replaces `for (string s : words)`.
- **Automatic cleanup:** your original trie never deletes its allocated nodes.

**Trade-off:** finding a child scans up to 26 siblings rather than doing one array lookup. With lowercase English letters, total time remains \(O(L)\) for \(L\) total input characters, but the lookup constant can be larger.

If speed matters more, a middle ground is a pooled node containing `std::array<int, 26>` and a count: typically **108 bytes per node**, with constant-time child access.

### Idea

Existing roads split the houses into connected components. We only need to connect these components.

For any two components, the cheapest road between them connects their cheapest houses.

Let `s` be the cheapest house in the entire city. For every component not containing `s`, build a road from its cheapest house to `s`.

### Why is this optimal?

Suppose there are `k` components. Since road costs are positive, an optimal solution connects them using a tree with `k - 1` roads.

Root this tree at the component containing `s`. For every other component `C`, its road to its parent costs at least

`price[cheapest house in C] + price[s]`.

Our construction achieves exactly this bound for every component, so its total cost is minimal.

### Complexity

- Time: `O((n + m) α(n))`, where `α` is the inverse Ackermann function.
- Extra space: `O(n)`.

### C++17

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct DSU {
    vector<int> parent, sz;

    DSU(int n) : parent(n + 1), sz(n + 1, 1) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int v) {
        if (parent[v] == v) return v;
        return parent[v] = find(parent[v]);
    }

    void unite(int a, int b) {
        a = find(a);
        b = find(b);

        if (a == b) return;

        if (sz[a] < sz[b]) swap(a, b);
        parent[b] = a;
        sz[a] += sz[b];
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<int> price(n + 1);
    int cheapest = 1;

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        cin >> price[i];
        if (price[i] < price[cheapest]) {
            cheapest = i;
        }
    }

    DSU dsu(n);

    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        dsu.unite(u, v);
    }

    // Cheapest house in each connected component.
    vector<int> best(n + 1, -1);

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        int root = dsu.find(i);
        if (best[root] == -1 || price[i] < price[best[root]]) {
            best[root] = i;
        }
    }

    int mainRoot = dsu.find(cheapest);
    vector<pair<int, int>> answer;

    for (int root = 1; root <= n; ++root) {
        if (best[root] != -1 && root != mainRoot) {
            answer.emplace_back(cheapest, best[root]);
        }
    }

    cout << answer.size() << '\n';
    for (auto [u, v] : answer) {
        cout << u << ' ' << v << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This returns the sum of **one element from each array** closest to `b`. A sum may exceed the budget; ties favor the smaller sum.

```js
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  if (!arr1.length || !arr2.length) return null;

  // Sort copies to avoid modifying the original arrays.
  const a = [...arr1].sort((x, y) => x - y);
  const c = [...arr2].sort((x, y) => x - y);

  let i = 0;
  let j = c.length - 1;
  let closest = a[i] + c[j];

  while (i < a.length && j >= 0) {
    const sum = a[i] + c[j];
    const diff = Math.abs(sum - b);
    const bestDiff = Math.abs(closest - b);

    if (diff < bestDiff || (diff === bestDiff && sum < closest)) {
      closest = sum;
    }

    if (sum === b) return sum;
    if (sum < b) i++;
    else j--;
  }

  return closest;
}

console.log(getExpense([3, 7, 10], [2, 6, 9], 15)); // 16
console.log(getExpense([3, 7, 10], [2, 6, 9], 13)); // 13
```

**Time complexity:** `O(n log n + m log m)` for sorting, followed by an `O(n + m)` scan.

可以使用 `sq_GetUniformVelocity` 对 X、Y、Z 坐标分别插值，实现平滑移动。下面设置 **200 毫秒**到达目标位置，可自行调整。

注意：这段移动期间不要再调用原来的 `sq_moveWithParent(parentChr, obj)`，也不要同时开启原来的自动跟随，否则可能覆盖插值位置。

