## 7. Build and test step by step

### Step 1 — Test the display and RTC

Connect only the Nano, OLED, and RTC, then upload the sketch.

The OLED should show approximately:

```text
HOUSE SIMULATOR
Time  06:00

Activity:
Morning news
Speed 6:1
Night: NORMAL 6h
TV L:-- B:ON
```

At the default ratio, the displayed clock should advance **one model minute every ten actual seconds**.

If the display is blank:

- Check VCC and GND.
- Check A4/SDA and A5/SCL.
- Confirm the display is SSD1306 rather than SH1106.
- Try address `0x3D` instead of `0x3C`, or run an I²C scanner.

If it says `RTC not found`, check the RTC wiring and confirm that it is a DS3231.

### Step 2 — Add the LEDs

Disconnect power before wiring.

Add the seven LEDs with their individual resistors. Power up again.

At 06:00:

- Kitchen light should be on.
- Hall light should be on.
- Bedroom TV should flicker.
- Other outputs should be off.

### Step 3 — Add the speed button

Connect the D3 pushbutton.

Starting from 6:1, successive presses select:

```text
12:1 → 24:1 → 60:1 → 1:1 → 3:1 → 6:1
```

At **60:1**, one model hour takes one actual minute, which makes testing much faster.

### Step 4 — Test bedtime

For a quick bedtime test, change:

```cpp
const uint8_t START_HOUR = 21;
const uint8_t START_MINUTE = 55;
```

Upload again.

You should see:

1. Bedroom light and bedroom TV on.
2. At 22:00, the bedroom light goes out.
3. The bedroom TV continues flickering alone.
4. At midnight, the bedroom TV goes out.

At 6:1, the interval from 22:00 to midnight takes **20 actual minutes**.

### Step 5 — Test short-night mode

Close the D2 switch.

After midnight:

- Everything remains off for one model hour.
- At 01:00, the model clock jumps to 06:00.
- Morning activity starts again.

If you close the switch while the clock is already between 01:00 and 06:00, it jumps immediately to 06:00. Returning to normal mode does not move time backward.

### Step 6 — Restore your desired start time

After testing, set the start time back to 06:00 or another preferred time.

---

## 8. Installing the lights in the model house

For a convincing appearance:

- Add opaque partitions between rooms so each window belongs to a distinct lighting zone.
- Paint or line thin walls internally to stop light bleeding through them.
- Put room LEDs near the ceiling, aimed at a pale surface for indirect illumination.
- Put each TV LED behind a tiny translucent rectangle representing the screen.
- Add a little blue tint or use a cool-white LED for the TV.
- Keep the TV effect dim enough that it suggests a screen rather than a flashing room light.

A single LED gives **brightness flicker**, not changing TV colors. An RGB LED can produce a richer effect, but requires additional outputs or an addressable-LED version of the project.

### Power-off behavior

The daily routine repeats indefinitely while powered. With the default settings:

- Every restart begins at **06:00 and 6:1**.
- The RTC keeps real time on its backup battery.
- The accelerated model time and selected speed are **not saved** when power is removed.

That makes this version predictable for operating sessions: turn it on, and the household begins its morning routine.

Move the API calls out of the spec into two layers:

- **API adapter:** Knows endpoints, payloads, and response contracts.
- **User provisioner:** Coordinates user creation and permission assignment.

Use a **Playwright fixture** to provide a provisioner per test and clean up created users—even when permission assignment or the test fails.

This applies the **Adapter pattern**, **Facade pattern**, and **dependency injection** without introducing an unnecessary inheritance hierarchy.

> The endpoints and payloads below are illustrative. In particular, adjust the delete endpoint and permission fields to match your API.

### 1. Define the contract

```typescript
// support/users/user-gateway.ts

export interface CreateUserInput {
  username: string;
  email: string;
}

export interface Credentials {
  username: string;
  password: string;
}

export interface ProvisionedUser extends Credentials {
  id: string;
}

export type Permission = "ADMIN" | "READ" | "WRITE";

export interface UserGateway {
  create(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<{ id: string }>;

  updatePermissions(
    userId: string,
    permissions: readonly Permission[],
  ): Promise<Credentials>;

  delete(userId: string): Promise<void>;
}
```

The provisioner depends on this interface, not on Playwright or endpoint details.

### 2. Implement the API adapter

```typescript
// support/users/playwright-user-api.ts

import type {
  APIRequestContext,
  APIResponse,
} from "@playwright/test";

import type {
  CreateUserInput,
  Credentials,
  Permission,
  UserGateway,
} from "./user-gateway";

export class PlaywrightUserApi implements UserGateway {
  constructor(private readonly request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async create(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<{ id: string }> {
    const response = await this.request.post("/users/v2/create", {
      data: input,
    });

    this.assertStatus(response, [201], "Create user");

    const body: unknown = await response.json();

    if (
      typeof body !== "object" ||
      body === null ||
      !("id" in body) ||
      typeof body.id !== "string" ||
      body.id.length === 0
    ) {
      throw new Error("Create user returned an invalid user ID");
    }

    return { id: body.id };
  }

  async updatePermissions(
    userId: string,
    permissions: readonly Permission[],
  ): Promise<Credentials> {
    const response = await this.request.post("/users/v2/permissions", {
      data: {
        id: userId,
        permissions: [...permissions],
      },
    });

    this.assertStatus(response, [200], "Update user permissions");

    const body: unknown = await response.json();

    if (
      typeof body !== "object" ||
      body === null ||
      !("username" in body) ||
      typeof body.username !== "string" ||
      !("password" in body) ||
      typeof body.password !== "string"
    ) {
      throw new Error("Update permissions returned invalid credentials");
    }

    return {
      username: body.username,
      password: body.password,
    };
  }

  async delete(userId: string): Promise<void> {
    const response = await this.request.delete(
      `/users/v2/${encodeURIComponent(userId)}`,
    );

    // Accept 404 because a CRUD test may already have deleted this user.
    this.assertStatus(response, [204, 404], "Delete user");
  }

  private assertStatus(
    response: APIResponse,
    expectedStatuses: readonly number[],
    operation: string,
  ): void {
    if (!expectedStatuses.includes(response.status())) {
      // Do not log response bodies that may contain credentials.
      throw new Error(
        `${operation} failed: expected HTTP ` +
          `${expectedStatuses.join(" or ")}, received ${response.status()}`,
      );
    }
  }
}
```

Notice the Playwright syntax: request payloads belong inside `{ data: payload }`.

### 3. Encapsulate the provisioning workflow

```typescript
// support/users/user-provisioner.ts

import type {
  CreateUserInput,
  Permission,
  ProvisionedUser,
  UserGateway,
} from "./user-gateway";

export class UserProvisioner {
  private readonly createdUserIds = new Set<string>();

  constructor(private readonly users: UserGateway) {}

  async create(
    input: CreateUserInput,
    permissions: readonly Permission[],
  ): Promise<ProvisionedUser> {
    const { id } = await this.users.create(input);

    // Track immediately: cleanup still runs if permission assignment fails.
    this.createdUserIds.add(id);

    const credentials = await this.users.updatePermissions(id, permissions);

    return {
      id,
      ...credentials,
    };
  }

  async createAdmin(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<ProvisionedUser> {
    return this.create(input, ["ADMIN"]);
  }

  async cleanup(): Promise<void> {
    // Attempt every deletion, even if an individual deletion fails.
    const results = await Promise.allSettled(
      [...this.createdUserIds].map(async (id) => {
        await this.users.delete(id);
        this.createdUserIds.delete(id);
      }),
    );

    const errors = results.flatMap((result) =>
      result.status === "rejected" ? [result.reason] : [],
    );

    if (errors.length > 0) {
      throw new AggregateError(errors, "Failed to clean up test users");
    }
  }
}
```

Additional setup calls—assigning groups, enabling features, or linking resources—belong in this workflow rather than in the spec.

